import{_ as s,C as r,o as n,c as a,j as t,a as i,E as h,a6 as d}from"./chunks/framework.CCKCzdWs.js";const k=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Runs started outside Piwi","description":"A Playwright run you start yourself, in a terminal or with a JetBrains IDE's own Run, follows the editor window whose workspace holds it, else the desktop app that links its folder: it stops at the editor's breakpoints and takes the same controls as a run Piwi started.","frontmatter":{"title":"Runs started outside Piwi","description":"A Playwright run you start yourself, in a terminal or with a JetBrains IDE's own Run, follows the editor window whose workspace holds it, else the desktop app that links its folder: it stops at the editor's breakpoints and takes the same controls as a run Piwi started.","lang":"en-US","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://piwitests.dev/features/terminal-runs.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://piwitests.dev/features/terminal-runs.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Runs started outside Piwi | Piwi for Playwright"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"A Playwright run you start yourself, in a terminal or with a JetBrains IDE's own Run, follows the editor window whose workspace holds it, else the desktop app that links its folder: it stops at the editor's breakpoints and takes the same controls as a run Piwi started."}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"features/terminal-runs.md","filePath":"features/terminal-runs.md","lastUpdated":1794510697000}`),l={name:"features/terminal-runs.md"};function u(p,e,w,c,f,g){const o=r("Needs");return n(),a("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"runs-started-outside-piwi",tabindex:"-1"},[i("Runs started outside Piwi "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#runs-started-outside-piwi","aria-label":'Permalink to "Runs started outside Piwi"'},"​")],-1)),h(o,{fixtures:""}),e[1]||(e[1]=d('<p>A run you start yourself follows the VS Code or JetBrains window whose workspace holds it: <code>npx playwright test</code> in a terminal, with a reporter and an editor extension newer than 0.62.0, or a JetBrains IDE&#39;s own Playwright run (the gutter&#39;s <strong>Run</strong> and <strong>Debug</strong>, a Playwright run configuration), with a reporter newer than 0.63.0. It stops at the editor&#39;s breakpoints, shows in the editor and in Piwi Picker&#39;s <a href="./runs-window.html">Runs window</a>, marked <strong>Terminal</strong> or <strong>Test runner</strong>, and takes the same controls (<a href="./editor-run-control.html">Pause and step from the editor</a>) but <strong>Stop</strong>: its process is the terminal&#39;s or the IDE&#39;s. It says so as it starts:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>[Piwi Dashboard] Following this run in Visual Studio Code (pid 41230), whose breakpoints stop it: PIWI_EDITOR=off runs it alone.</span></span></code></pre></div><p>With no editor window on its folder, a run in a folder <a href="./desktop.html">the desktop app</a> links follows the app (a reporter newer than 0.68.0): its Local runs tray lists it, pauses it and watches its workers, as it does <a href="./desktop-runs.html">the runs it starts</a>, and Piwi Picker&#39;s Runs window holds its failures as you ask.</p><p>It runs as its config says, headless by default, so the editor shows a stop as <a href="./editor-run-control.html#a-headless-run">a headless run&#39;s</a>: an image of its page, which the editor&#39;s paused page and Piwi Picker&#39;s Runs window can watch live. To see the browser, add <code>--headed</code> to the command, or to the Playwright options of the run configuration.</p><h2 id="which-editor-or-the-desktop-app" tabindex="-1">Which editor, or the desktop app <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-editor-or-the-desktop-app" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which editor, or the desktop app&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each editor window&#39;s Piwi service writes a file in <code>~/.piwi/editors</code> (<code>PIWI_EDITORS_DIR</code> names another folder) with its workspace folders and where its runs connect. The run follows the window one of whose folders holds its Playwright config&#39;s folder or its working directory, links resolved: the most specific folder wins, then the window that started last. A window of another machine, another user&#39;s, or one whose process ended is never followed, nor is a file written before the machine last started, any file of a folder another user can open, or (but on Windows) a window whose runs would connect through a folder another user can open.</p><p>No window holding it, the run follows the desktop app whose linked folder holds it: the app names where its runs connect in <code>~/.piwi/desktop.json</code> (<code>PIWI_DESKTOP_CONFIG</code> names another file) once its server is up, by the same rules.</p><h2 id="when-it-follows-one" tabindex="-1">When it follows one <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-it-follows-one" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When it follows one&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A run whose output goes to a terminal, or that a test runner started</strong> (one that names its reporter&#39;s file in <code>PW_TEST_REPORTER</code>, as a JetBrains IDE&#39;s Playwright runner does), is attended: it stops at the breakpoints.</li><li><strong>A run whose output goes to a file or a pipe</strong>, as an agent&#39;s, follows unattended: it is listed and <strong>Pause</strong> stops it, but no breakpoint does and no failure waits. <code>PIWI_EDITOR=on</code> attends it. It says so as it starts (<a href="./watch-a-run.html#the-terminal-s-lines">the terminal&#39;s lines</a>).</li><li><strong>Never</strong> under CI, with Playwright&#39;s own debugger (<code>--debug</code>, <code>PWDEBUG=1</code>) or UI mode, when the run names its own <code>PIWI_RUN_CHANNEL</code>, or when the desktop app or another launcher started it.</li><li><strong><code>PIWI_EDITOR=off</code></strong> runs it alone.</li></ul><h2 id="what-it-takes-from-the-editor" tabindex="-1">What it takes from the editor <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-takes-from-the-editor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What it takes from the editor&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The breakpoints.</strong> It starts with the editor&#39;s breakpoints as they are, then takes them as you change them, while <code>piwi.breakpoints</code> (<strong>Pause the runs Piwi starts at the editor&#39;s breakpoints</strong> in a JetBrains IDE) is on. Without any, it runs as it would, and <strong>Pause</strong> stops it.</li><li><strong>The controls</strong>: Pause, Resume, Step, Run to cursor, Finish and <a href="./end-the-test.html">End the test</a>, and the code a stop makes, Assert a region and Mock a request, written in the editor (<a href="./editor-stop-code.html">Code from a pause</a>).</li><li><strong>Record here and Assert</strong> in the test&#39;s window, through the editor&#39;s recorder, and <strong>Pick a locator</strong>, which goes to the editor (its Send to editor pairing), as for a run Piwi starts.</li><li><strong>Its stops on the desktop app&#39;s page of the run</strong>, for a run that reports to <a href="./desktop.html">the app</a>: which tests wait for the editor (<strong>In Visual Studio Code</strong>), each place opening in the IDE.</li><li><strong>Failures.</strong> A failure goes on, as without Piwi, until you turn the run&#39;s <a href="/guide/concepts.html#hold-failures"><strong>Hold failures</strong></a> on, in the editor or the Runs window: nobody may be there to answer it otherwise.</li></ul><p>When the editor closes, or its service stops, the connection closes, and a stop with no pause bar goes on. The token of the editor&#39;s connection reaches the run&#39;s workers alone, out of the tests&#39; environment while they run, as for a run the editor starts.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Under the desktop app, a pick comes to its window to copy</strong>, and Record here and Assert, which need an editor, are not offered. An editor extension older than this one names no pairing for the run: start the test from the editor to pick there.</li><li><strong>No Stop.</strong> End the run in its terminal, or with the IDE&#39;s own <strong>Stop</strong>.</li><li><strong>Debug stops twice.</strong> Under a JetBrains IDE&#39;s <strong>Debug</strong>, a breakpoint stops the test in the debugger, then at Piwi&#39;s stop, whose controls take no key while the debug session runs. <code>PIWI_EDITOR=off</code> in the run configuration&#39;s environment leaves the breakpoints to the debugger.</li><li><strong>Piwi&#39;s reporter in the config.</strong> The run looks for an editor as the reporter starts (<code>wrapConfig</code> adds it): with the capture fixtures alone, it runs alone.</li><li><strong>Your machine.</strong> A run in a dev container or over SSH follows an editor whose service runs there too; the desktop app&#39;s folders are this machine&#39;s alone.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./editor-run-control.html">Pause and step from the editor</a>: the controls, and the paused line.</li><li><a href="./editor-breakpoints.html">Breakpoints in the browser</a>: the breakpoints a run takes.</li><li><a href="./editor-runs.html">Runs from the editor</a>: the runs Piwi starts.</li><li><a href="./runs-window.html">Runs window</a>: the same runs, in Piwi Picker.</li><li><a href="./desktop-runs.html">Desktop app runs</a>: the Local runs tray, which follows a run in a linked folder.</li></ul>',16))])}const P=s(l,[["render",u]]);export{k as __pageData,P as default};
